why do you like twist endings huh I uh I  guess I love them as a kid growing up I  loved trying to uh you know see the  twist coming trying to solve it  um I you know I rarely could as a kid  and I just love the idea of of your your  expectations and your experience kind of  being turned on its head and with  something else all along I love that and  I've  um on Ghost Whisperer we started  developing this idea of twists with with  you know every episode and then twist  upon twists and all kinds of things and  so I really got uh very comfortable with  it in that and loved it and now in these  short films  um I'm doing it all the time I just uh  you know almost all of them have had at  least one twist I usually try to get  three twists in to really confuse the  audience of it they don't see it coming  and the short film it's particularly  difficult because you have so little  time to lay the pipe for for a Twist and  so the audience and you know if they  know there's going to be a Twist they  know they're watching this film because  it can't possibly just be this there's  got to be something else going on with  this character  um so you have to it's a delicate  balance of having to try to redirect the  audience  and sort of have a red hairy twist that  they think it's going to be and then the  actual twist which they can never see  coming and every movie I do it becomes  harder and harder to come up with that  Twist of you know well we never saw that  coming but it's fun it's a great  challenge to do it why would you say  it's become harder is that because so  much has been done  so much has been done and you know  there's a thing of trying to top  yourself uh and again it's the short  film sort of format  and um uh and sometimes I work I just  start with the ending if I'm trying to  develop a Twist I start with the ending  and then I go backwards  um how how many convolutions can I take  away from this ending that that makes it  a surprise it makes it a Twist or yeah  and then by doing it backwards  something that I can write a script in a  linear way beginning to end with one  final twist  but then I'll go backwards and think  about where else can I put another twist  where is there oh look in scene three I  could plant this seed that No One's  Gonna really notice but then it's going  to pay off in this scene and then when  the twist happens everyone will be an  aha moment of oh that's why those things  were there uh so it's just it's a it's a  mechanical thing in a way it's like a  Craftsman kind of thing to to do a good  twist  and so now I've just I kind of love them  and every time I write a short film I  become terrified I'm not gonna be able  to find a good twist or enough of them  what is the Hollywood ending and what's  wrong with the Hollywood ending  I don't know what that means today you  know I mean I think at a certain point  in film history it referred to the idea  of everyone is happy everything's tied  up in a bow and you know your leave the  theater completely satisfied and  everything's good and yeah there's  certainly nothing wrong with that and  that's certainly what a lot of people go  to the movies for uh they don't you know  they don't want to have enough  depression and unhappiness in their  lives they don't need to see it on  screen but um for an artist that's  normally just an unsatisfying way to  work and uh you're for most writers and  directors and actors you  um you know you you just want to do work  that reflects life a little more it  doesn't have to be not uplifting but uh  you know realistic I just wanted to be a  little more realistic and I think that  the idea of a Hollywood ending it's not  really uh prevalent that much anymore I  think because of the streaming you know  influx of streaming services and digital  everything I think audiences are growing  more sophisticated  and are more willing to accept a  convoluted ending or you know a more  complex ending and uh I mean I haven't  gotten a note like that in years about  you know about an ending to make it more  palatable or more Hollywood  how do you pull off a twist ending well  it's it's really like a magician's trick  it's about misdirection  it's about telling the audience to look  over here when what I'm really doing is  over here  and it's really tricky because you were  just talking about how sophisticated  audiences are today so you know they see  it coming right away so you really got  to be careful and what I try to do with  like a you know a triple twist kind of a  thing is I set something up  that the audience right away feels like  oh that's what this is what it's going  to be it's going to be I know it I can  tell right away  and you you play that along and then at  the same time I try to introduce what I  would call the red herring twist  which is a little thing that you you you  weave into a scene that you do in such a  way that the audience is going to notice  it  and they're going to think that they  just caught you ah this is why this  guy's there because in the end it's  going to be that and you play that and  if you've seen you you play those  expectations and you try to disguise it  uh like I'm being clever now and I'm  gonna try to pull this over on you but  you're too smart for me  and in the meantime there's a third  thing that just comes out of the blue  that they never saw coming it's been  there the whole time because you can't  just do a these ex machina you just you  can't just you've got to have it woven  in but it's something that they never  really thought about as as something's  going to pay off  so you know this sounds very esoteric  it's it becomes more obvious in the  actual execution of it but that's my  that's the way I do it you know and like  oftentimes I'll my first pass on the  script I'll do the obvious  Choice and then the the red herring  Choice and then I gotta really struggle  to figure okay how is it even different  even another Twist on this twist that we  plant in the beginning that no one  realizes we've been tracking this whole  time  you know so it's it's fun it's great fun  to pull it off and it's what's wonderful  about it and I love some about my short  films is to sit in an audience you know  in a theater uh at a film festival and  you have people react to those twists  it's fantastic and it's one of the  things I love about short films by the  way  um because of my TV work I didn't I was  a connected to the audience  um you know everyone's watching on TV in  their home and I don't know but uh on  the film festival circuit uh where I'm a  regular with these short films it's  phenomenal and people seem to love short  films these these screenings are packed  and we get these big theaters and it's  it's about as good experience as you can  have making a movie watching your story  unfold even if it's just 10 minutes uh  with an audience that you know that gets  it is there a director or writer  director that you remember from the  beginning watching movies back in in  Brooklyn that was the master of the  twist ending  well you know I mean I guess Hitchcock  of course comes to mind you know  immediately  um  but uh I I think I became more enamored  as a kid of the twist ending in  literature in things I would read  and you know you're so much uh you have  so much more opportunity to do twists in  fiction because the your readers can't  see anything  so you could be describing a character  or a location uh in a way that can  disguise the fact that it's really this  or this person is really that person the  unreliable narrator but you don't have  that luxury uh with a movie because  they're gonna they're seeing everything  you're doing  um so it's you're that much more  challenging but the answer to the  question is as a kid I remember  um you know reading detective fiction  and and that kind of stuff and really  being drawn in by a good twist that I  didn't see coming  should the end of a story always tie  back to the beginning  uh I don't know if if it always yeah  like a rule um uh I think is great if it  does it's a great thing visually to do  and uh I think it gives the audience  more of a feeling that they've done this  complete Journey  um  you know I did it not to a granddose  myself you know but uh I I did a movie  Once about the relationship between  Jerry Lewis and Dean Martin  and I began the movie in Dean Martin's  dressing room  on a close-up of a glass of scotch with  ice cubes in it  and I held that for a long time in the  background you could hear a nightclub  because some muffled comic and people  laughing and hooting and and that hand  came into the frame and took the drink  and we revealed Dean Martin  and at the end of the movie when they  break up  uh it also happens in this time in  Jerry's dressing room  and they're saying goodbye they just  said they have farewell performance of  the Copacabana we've gone through it's  like 10 years of their lives  and when Dean says goodbye and they just  have the very awkward kind of parting  full of pain talk about subtext you know  they're dying inside each one of them  but neither one can talk about it they  can just do these perfunctory good show  yeah best one okay good take care yeah  meantime it's like oh don't leave me  um so we did the scene and then  afterwards I leave with Jerry just  looking in the mirror when Dean's walked  away and I pan down and I I find a glass  of scotch just mirror the first shot you  know totally and so I mean that's just  an example again not that like you know  but it was I'm such a genius but those  are really great visual ways to tie  things in and I had to fight with the  network about keeping that you know cut  on his face just cut out on his face no  no there's a reason uh but anytime you  can tie in the beginning to the end or  any other part of the movie you know  callbacks are always yeah they'll you  know the joke in movies you know you can  never pull a gun on screen without  having that gun play somehow in the  movie you can't show it you can and I  feel like that's true about everything  in the movie you can't introduce  anything in a film that doesn't somehow  call back later and even if it's a  character  um you know everything in the movie has  to be there for a reason and I think it  teaches you a great discipline in  writing if you try to apply that  everything you write okay I'm going to  introduce this prop this this person is  this event and it can't be just there  for the sake of being there it's got to  be there to serve the story and so how  do we call it back later on  and callbacks were different than  bookending  I suppose so I mean I think they can be  the same but they could also be  different in that you know a callback uh  it just could be just even just a fun  thing where we we see uh a joke  introduced at the beginning of a film  someone always loses their glove and  then uh you know a third of the way  through the film that that comes up  again uh that lose the glove  um and again it's an example of like  never like something go to waste don't  don't introduce someone who loses the  glove and then not somehow bring them  back later not pay that off in some way  uh so you know I think if you have to be  strict strict about it you know bookends  obviously being in the end of a film and  callbacks can just happen anywhere in  the body of a movie but also I don't  want to feel like there's rules like  that they really aren't it's just about  what what you feel works for your story
 

